## darvis/livewire-flux-editor-filemanager

Laravel Filemanager inside the Flux Pro editor: toolbar buttons that open the file manager to insert images and file links, a resize and align menu and an edit modal on images, link editing, an HTML source modal, a table menu, and drag and drop or paste of image files.

@verbatim
- Use `<x-flux-filemanager-editor wire:model="content" />` where you would use `<flux:editor>`. Attributes: `toolbar` and `id`; everything else is passed to `<flux:editor>`. `toolbar` is a preset name (`minimal`, `default` or `full`), a Flux toolbar string such as `toolbar="heading | bold italic | image link file-link"` (`|` is a separator, `~` a spacer, and a preset name inside it expands to its items: `toolbar="default | html-source"`), or `false` with your own `<flux:editor.toolbar>` in the slot. The package buttons are Flux toolbar items by name, in that string and as `<flux:editor.image />` in a Blade toolbar: `image`, `file-link`, `html-source`, `hr`, `code-block`, `clear-format`, `table` and `checklist`; an unknown name throws "Flux component [editor.<name>] does not exist.". `rows` is only printed as an HTML attribute and does not change the height; set the height with a class such as `**:data-[slot=content]:min-h-[300px]!`.
- `hr`, `code-block` and every `table-*` command are run by Flux's own toolbar click handler, `clear-format` by the package JavaScript (`clearNodes().unsetAllMarks()` on the selection). Never add JavaScript for `hr`, `code-block` or `table-*`: the command would run twice. The checklist button is in no preset and opens a 404 unless `demo_routes` is on; add it only while setting up locally, `toolbar="full | checklist"`.
- The `table` button (in the `full` preset, with `toolbar="default | table"`, or as `<flux:editor.table />` in a Blade toolbar) is a menu: insert a 3×3 table with a header row, add or delete rows and columns, header row and header column on or off, merge or split cells, delete the table. Its trigger carries `data-editor="table-menu"`, which Flux ignores; the items carry `table`, `table-add-row-before`, `table-add-row-after`, `table-delete-row`, `table-add-column-before`, `table-add-column-after`, `table-delete-column`, `table-toggle-header-row`, `table-toggle-header-column`, `table-merge-or-split` and `table-delete`, and Flux's editor runs those commands.
- Tables need Flux Pro 2.15.1 or newer, which the package requires since 1.10.0, and the four `enableExtension` lines in the setup block of `app.js`. `php artisan flux-filemanager:install` adds them to an existing block, and `flux-filemanager:check` warns when they are missing (the check "resources/js/app.js enables Flux's table extensions"). Without them the button is disabled and the console warns "Flux Filemanager: the table extensions are not enabled, so the table button is disabled." Never install the table extensions through npm or register them with `registerExtension()`: Flux Pro bundles them, and a second copy of ProseMirror breaks the editor. Flux's own editor stylesheet draws the tables in the editor (borders, header cells, selected cells, the resize handle, dark mode); the package's `tiptap-table.css` (imported by `tiptap-image.css`) only zeroes the margin of the paragraph in a cell and left-aligns header cells. The public page needs its own table CSS, or Tailwind Typography's `prose`.
- The buttons only work when `resources/js/app.js` registers the extensions and calls `initLaravelFilemanager()`; the table button also needs the block to enable Flux's bundled table extensions (`e.detail.enableExtension('table')`, `tableRow`, `tableHeader` and `tableCell`). `php artisan flux-filemanager:install` writes that block; `examples/app.js` in the package shows the complete file. Never give the Image extension its own node view or `resize: true`: Flux's editor already renders images with one, and two node views break selection.
- Import the package's JavaScript as a namespace (`import * as fluxFilemanager from '../../vendor/darvis/livewire-flux-editor-filemanager/resources/js/laravel-filemanager.js'`) and call the plugin factory with `?.()`. Never convert it to named imports: one missing export is a module error, and that stops all of `app.js`, so every toolbar button dies at once.
- Every button dead at the same time is an `app.js` problem, not a component problem: read the browser console first. Right after a package update, restart `npm run dev` before looking further, because Vite does not watch `vendor/` and keeps serving the JavaScript it read at startup.
- Run `php artisan flux-filemanager:check` before you start reading code when something doesn't work. It checks the config, the routes, the authentication on the file manager, the storage link, the npm packages, `app.js` (the setup block and the table lines) and the build, and prints what to do. It needs no demo routes and exits non-zero when something is broken; missing table lines are a warning, not a failure.
- The HTML button (`data-editor="html-source"`: in the `full` preset, with `toolbar="default | html-source"`, or as `<flux:editor.html-source />` in a Blade toolbar) opens the HTML of the content in a modal; Apply calls `setContent()`, so tags and attributes without an extension are dropped, and the hint under the text area names them (`<iframe>`, `<script>`, `p[style]`) while the user types; `<table>` survives since 1.10 when the table extensions are enabled. Flux's own `</>` button is the inline `code` mark and shows nothing without a selection; when someone reports a broken "view code" button, that is the one they clicked, and the HTML button is what they want.
- The content is HTML with `<img>` tags (classes `tiptap-image` and `align-*`, inline width and margins, `data-align`), `<a>` tags with `target` and `rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow"`, and `<table>` with `<tbody>`, `<tr>`, `<th>` and `<td>` (`colspan`, `rowspan`, a `<p>` in every cell). The package does not sanitise it; render it unescaped only for trusted editors.
- Settings live in `config/flux-filemanager.php` (`url`, `popup`, `resize_presets`, `custom_width`, `messages`, `drag_drop`). The component renders them and the translations as JSON for the JavaScript; never hardcode the file manager URL or texts in `app.js`.
- Dropped and pasted images are base64 by default. Set `drag_drop.method` to `upload` to post them to Laravel Filemanager; `max_file_size` and `allowed_types` apply to both, but they are checked in the browser only. On the server only `config/lfm.php` counts (`valid_mime`, `should_validate_size`), and its default image types do not include WebP or SVG.
- The demo pages `/darvis/editor-demo` and `/darvis/filemanager-checklist` exist only when `FLUX_FILEMANAGER_DEMO_ROUTES=true`. They have no auth; keep them off in production.
- Protect the file manager itself with `middlewares` in `config/lfm.php`; the package adds no authentication and the editor only opens `/filemanager`. `auth` admits every logged-in user, so add a stricter middleware when visitors can register. Image URLs come from `APP_URL`, so it must match the host in use.
- In tests, render the component with `$this->blade()` after sharing an empty `ViewErrorBag` as `errors`; Flux reads `$errors` and there is no web middleware there.

<code-snippet name="Editor in a Livewire form" lang="blade">
<flux:field>
    <flux:label>Content</flux:label>
    <x-flux-filemanager-editor wire:model="content" toolbar="full" :rows="15" />
    <flux:error name="content" />
</flux:field>
</code-snippet>
@endverbatim
